 "drivingQuestion": "What is the kasva, and where is the zealots' license hinted?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "term defined and source given",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.81b.18",
   "daf": "81b",
   "sentenceId": 17,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מַאי קַסְוָה? אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה: כְּלִי שָׁרֵת. וְכֵן הוּא אוֹמֵר: ״וְאֶת קְשׂוֹת הַנָּסֶךְ״. וְהֵיכָא רְמִיזָא? ״וְלֹא יָבֹאוּ לִרְאוֹת כְּבַלַּע אֶת הַקֹּדֶשׁ וָמֵתוּ״.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The Gemara asks: «What» is the «kasva» mentioned in the mishna? «Rav Yehuda says:» It is a term used to describe any «service vessel» utilized in the Temple. «And likewise, it is stated: “And the jars [kesot] with which to pour libations”» (Numbers 4:7). The Gemara asks: «And where is it intimated» that one who steals a service vessel is liable to be killed by zealots? The Gemara answers that it is intimated in the verse: «“But they shall not come to see the sacred items as they are being covered [kevala], lest they die”» (Numbers 4:20). The Gemara interprets the term kevala as one who ingests, i.e., who takes (see Job 20:15), and the verse thereby teaches that one who steals a service vessel is liable to be killed."
  }
 ],
